CORE
main.cpp
--cpp14
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ function: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ lambda: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.3\] line \d+ WRONG \(must FAIL\): FAILURE$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
^CONVERSION ERROR$
--
N5008 [dcl.spec.auto.general]/10, [expr.rel]/2, [expr.eq]/1: `return true;'
and `return st == 0;' both deduce bool.  The front end deduced the
comparison as its internal __CPROVER_bool and rejected the function
("inconsistent deduction for the return type: 'bool' and '__CPROVER_bool'",
bin53); placeholder deduction, decltype and template argument deduction now
see bool (cpp_bool_type).  libc++'s __stop_state::__add_callback has such a
lambda, so its body was dropped (__stop_token/stop_state.h:140, for
<stop_token>, <thread>, <future> and <condition_variable>).
test_deduced_bool.desc: auto, decltype, deduced T and overloads.  g++ 13 and clang++ 18 accept
this program (-std=c++14), and it fails only its WRONG assertion.
